---
import LessonLayout from '../../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
const lessonRef = "CCC 1730-1748";
const lessonScripture = "Gal 5:1,13; Jn 8:32; Rom 6:18; Sir 15:14-17; 2 Cor 3:17";
---
<LessonLayout title="Freedom &mdash; Workbook" cic={lessonRef} scripture={lessonScripture}>

<section class="content">

<h2>Reviewing the Essentials</h2>

<ol>
<li>According to the Catechism, what is the definition of freedom? (CCC 1730)</li>
<li>What is the relationship between freedom and responsibility?</li>
<li>Name at least four factors that can diminish a person's freedom.</li>
<li>What did Jesus mean when he said "the truth will set you free"?</li>
<li>What is the Church's teaching on religious liberty?</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>For Reflection and Writing</h2>

<div class="question-block">
<p><strong>1.</strong> Describe a time in your life when you felt genuinely free. What made that moment different from ordinary moments? Did it involve doing whatever you wanted, or was it something else?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p><strong>2.</strong> "Freedom is not the ability to do whatever I want; it's the power to choose the good." Do you agree or disagree with this statement? Why?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p><strong>3.</strong> Imagine you are talking to a friend who says: "The Church has too many rules. I want to be free." How would you respond using what you've learned about true freedom?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p><strong>4.</strong> St. Thomas More chose death over betraying his conscience. Is there a situation in your life where you're tempted to compromise your beliefs because of social pressure? What would it take to stand firm?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p><strong>5.</strong> Galatians 5:1 says: "For freedom Christ set us free." From what has Christ set you free? What still holds you back?</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Practical Activities</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Freedom audit:</strong> Take a piece of paper and draw two columns. In the left column, list habits, attachments, and obligations that make you feel trapped or enslaved. In the right column, list the things that make you feel free. Compare the two lists. What do you notice?</li>
<li><strong>Read Galatians 5:</strong> Read the entire chapter slowly. Underline every mention of freedom, slavery, and the Spirit. Spend five minutes journaling about what the Holy Spirit wants to set you free from.</li>
<li><strong>One small step:</strong> What is one thing you've been putting off because of fear or laziness? Do it this week — not because you "should," but as an act of freedom.</li>
<li><strong>Pray the Suscipe:</strong> Pray St. Ignatius of Loyola's Suscipe prayer (Take, Lord, and receive all my liberty...) every morning this week.</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Memory Verse</h2>

<blockquote>
<p>"You will know the truth, and the truth will set you free."</p>
<cite>John 8:32</cite>
</blockquote>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Prayer for This Week</h2>

<p>
Lord Jesus, you came to set the captives free. Break every chain that binds my heart — fear, habit, sin, attachment to what is passing. Help me understand that your law is not a burden but a path to true freedom. Teach me to use my freedom not as an excuse for selfishness but as an opportunity to love. Give me the courage of St. Thomas More — to be the king's good servant but God's first.</p>

<p><em>Amen.</em></p>

</section>

